From: Alex Gorbachev <gorbachev_alex () bah ! com>
Date: Thu, 26 Feb 1998 18:28:35 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: Shared SCSI w/o ASE
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96078694111617

I have deployed a pair of 2 4100 servers with Digital UNIX connected
over a shared SCSI bus to a dual-redundant pair of HSZ50s with 4
RAIDsets.  The storage volumes are mounted each on only one system at a
time.  However, the systems perform a large number of SCSI bus resets,
which cause problems every once in a while (one KZPSA resets the bus and
the reset propagated in "waves" of error messages to the other system
and the LUNs).

DEC strongly feels that this is configuration related.

The question:

Is this never going to work and ASE is the only choice or can the
systems become more tolerant using a kernel fix, etc. to prevent SCSI
resets?

Any info would be greatly appreciated.
Alex Gorbachev
gorbachev_alex@bah.com

From tru64-unix-managers  Thu Feb 26 17:51:01 1998
From: William Flett <will () dcs ! rhbnc ! ac ! uk>
Date: Thu, 26 Feb 1998 17:51:01 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: SUMMARY: DEC NIS master and SUN clients
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96078694111616


Original query:

We are unable be get SUN Solaris systems to bind with a DEC master NIS
server running DU4.0B (patch level 6).  The error given on the SUN 
systems is "can't communicate with ypbind".

Summary:

As suggested by Angela MacKenzie the problem lay in SUN land.  The Solaris
systems NIS/ypbind had to be patched.  We applied both Solaris 2.5.1 and 2.6
patches kits (105165-02/103612-38 and 105403-01/105401-05 respectively).
After changing the NIS domains and rebooting every worked fine. 

One word of warning SUN have a job creation scheme which means that these
patches are only available on request.  In other words there not on the
SUN official patch site and you'll have to open a support call, then the
kind engineer will give you access to your personal copies (don't share them
now).  DEC people, think yourselves lucky.

