From tru64-unix-managers  Tue Feb 10 21:43:52 1998
From: "system PRIVILEGED account- John F. Marten" <root () tigger ! oslc ! org>
Date: Tue, 10 Feb 1998 21:43:52 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: SUMMARY: file dates
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96078694111243

thanx! to alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com who wrote:

	Dates are stored in a field which is at least 32 bits
	and in some file system implementation may be 64 bits.
	The type of this field (for programming use) is time_t.
	The value in field is the number of seconds since January
	1, 1970 00:00:00 GMT.  The value should be the same on
	ALL UNIX systems.

	If zoo is using time_t correctly, there shouldn't be any
	problem.


orig. message:
preface: before we migrated from an ultrix box our users used a =
compression=20
utility called zoo. we have a lot of data stored in zoo=20
format. there is a problem with the way zoo works on DU4.0b. either =
compressing=20
or uncompressing the dates on the files become mangled. i mean to say =
they when=20
uncompressing they don't show the date of the file when it was last =
worked on.=20
and compressing sometimes the files get tagged with a totaly bogus date. =
i'm=20
thinking the problem has to do with how the AS1000A 5/333 stores file =
dates.
question: Any info on how the Alpha stores the file dates?
thanx!

